Subject: [PATCH 62/62] forth/device: $call-method and $call-parent refuse an ihandle of 0 by name instead of reading address 0 as an instance

Arch-tested: x86 amd64 ppc unix

An ihandle of 0 is not an instance. $call-method read one as if it were --
`dup >r >in.device-node @ find-method` on 0 fetches the cell at address
0 + >in.device-node -- and what sits at address 0 depends on the arch. On amd64
it is the real-mode interrupt vector table: the "phandle" comes back as
f000ff54f000ff7b and find-method takes a general protection fault. On x86 the
same read happened to land on bytes that walked to "not found" and threw -21:
the right answer, by luck.

MEASURED 2026-09-03, before this patch, two ways:

    s" x" 0 ['] $call-method catch        amd64: GPF @ 08:0000000000102d93,
                                                 rax=f000ff54f000ff7b
                                          x86:   RC=-21 (luck)

    dev /pci8086,1237@0/pci1b36,1@3       (QEMU's pci-bridge)
    0 to my-self  active-package create-instance to my-self
    0 s" config-l@" my-self ['] $call-method catch
                                          amd64: GPF, same pc, same rax

The second is how it was first met: the 2026-09-03 audit of patches 56-59 built
a ONE-level instance for a card behind a bridge, the bridge's C method chained
$call-parent (patch 59), and the bridge instance's my-parent was 0. The audit
rebuilt the instance chain root-first in dsl/optrom.fth and named the fault in
TODO as "$call-parent from a parentless instance GPFs rather than throwing".
This is that fix. ?my-self already refuses my-self = 0 with "no current
instance." -- the same shape, for the other two zeros:

    $call-method   dup 0= abort" $call-method: ihandle is 0 (no instance)."
    $call-parent   my-parent dup 0= abort" no parent instance."

A caller's `catch` now gets a throw on every arch, and a driver written as if
it had an instance is told what it lacks. The optrom track types both probes
on x86 and amd64 and asserts a non-zero catch result, no "Exception" line, and
the message by name.

diff --git a/forth/device/package.fs b/forth/device/package.fs
--- a/forth/device/package.fs
+++ b/forth/device/package.fs
@@ -82,7 +82,18 @@
 ;
 
 
+\ mklab (patch 62): an ihandle of 0 is not an instance, and reading one as if
+\ it were -- `0 >in.device-node @` -- fetches whatever sits at address 0. On
+\ amd64 that is the real-mode interrupt vector table, and the "phandle" it yields
+\ (f000ff54f000ff7b) sends find-method through a general protection fault; on
+\ x86 the same read happened to hit bytes that walked to -21 by luck. Measured
+\ 2026-09-03 with `s" x" 0 $call-method` and, the way it was first met, with a
+\ card behind a PCI-PCI bridge whose bridge instance had my-parent = 0: the
+\ bridge's C method chains $call-parent (patch 59) and the firmware faulted
+\ instead of throwing. ?my-self already refuses my-self = 0 by name; these two
+\ refuse the other two zeros the same way, so a caller's `catch` gets a throw.
 : $call-method  ( ... method-str method-len ihandle -- ??? )
+  dup 0= abort" $call-method: ihandle is 0 (no instance)."
   dup >r >in.device-node @ find-method if
     r> call-package
   else
@@ -91,7 +102,8 @@
 ;
 
 : $call-parent  ( ... method-str method-len -- ??? )
-  my-parent $call-method
+  my-parent dup 0= abort" no parent instance."
+  $call-method
 ;
 
 
